{"text": "Reprint\neater \u00a7 Kaa AG. at\n\norm Re 1908:96-80 FILE cory 3P2f\n\nFrom the Neuroendocrinology of Lymphocytes toward a Molecular\nBasis of the Network Theory\n\nDaniel JJ. Carr, J. Edwin Blalock\nUniversity of Alabama at Birmingham, Department of Physiology and Biophysics, UAB Suton, Birwingham, Ala, USA\n\n| Hormone Research\ntoe 3 Grad ae!\n\nKey Words. Molecular recognition bypothesis - Neuroendocrine-immune axis - ACTH recepter \u00ab\nid receptor\n\nAbstract. The cells of the immune system produce and respond to hormones that were once thought to be\nrestricted to the neuroendocrine system. By applying a novel methodology based on the molecular recognition\n\u2018hypotbesis, the isolation and purification of receptors shared between the immuve and neuroendocrine systems was\naccomplished. Biochemical analysis revealed them to be virtually identical with respect to their physicochemical gad\nfunctional properties. Thus, bidirectional communication between the immune and neuroendocrine systems seems\nto result from a common set of hormones and receptors which are shared by the two systems. Furthermore, the\nmolecular recognition hypothesis has revealed that bomologues of the binding sites of these same hormone receptor\nairs may be contained within the bypervariable regions of immunoglobulins and therefore constitute part of the\n\nimmunologic network.\n\nIntroduction\n\nUntit recently, the immune system was believed to be\na self-contained, endogenously regulated unit modulated\nby cytokines and an immunologic network of idiotypic\nand anti-idiotypic antibodies. However, with the obser-\nvation that various neuropeptides modulate leukocyte\nFunctions came ah integration of the immune system\nwith other physiologic circuits [1]. A raison d\u2019\u00e9tre for the\nresponsivity of leukocytes to many neuroendocrine pep-\nides {for review. see ref, 2] became evident with the dis-\ncovery that cells of the immune system (immunocytes)\nProduce a host of neuroendocrine hormones including\ncorticotropin (ACTH), endorphins, enkephalins, thyro-\ntropin (TSH), somatotropin, prolactin, chorionic gonad-\n\u2018otropin and luteinizing hormone (for review, see ref. 3)\nWhile immunostionulants, such as viruses and mitogens,\nwere the first recognized inducers of these hormones,\n\u2018more recent observations have shown that immunocytes\nrespond with fidelity to hypothalamic releasing factors.\n\nPUBLICATIONS\n\n\u2018Tous, ACTH-reteasing factor stimulated a texameths\nsone-suppressible production of ACTH and endorphins\n[4] while TSH-releasing bormone caused TSH synthesis\nand release {5]. Collectively, these observations illustrate\n1 very similar behavior of neuroendocrine cells and\nimmunocytes. OF course, the findings that such hor-\nmones are endogenous components of the immune sys-\ntem make it much less surprising that immunocytes are\nacted upon by neuroendocrine peptides. These studies\nhave led to the ides that bidirectional communication\nbetween the immune and neuroendocrine systems re-\nsults from a common set of hormones and receptors\n\u2018which are shared by the two systems [1]. While there is\nlittle doubt that iramunecytes produce bona fide neu-\nrorndocrine hormones, a completion of this proposed\nbiochemical circuit would seem to require an ia depth\nknowledge of the hormone receptors which reside on leu-\nkocytes, To this end, we have applied a novel technology\nbased on the molecular recognition theory to study such\nreceptors.\n\n005057\n\n10323800",
